#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199318} :activity/id #uuid "741f534f-c497-52b3-a525-05d9bf7f9e8f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050224 :topic/id #uuid "37969d71-54e0-5479-8db0-03f8bc2bcfc3" :topic/math-academy-id 4160} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199265 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198368 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198242 :question/id #uuid "366c826f-6f15-5a21-9eaa-d74b6e4d8833" :question/math-academy-id "e-9575"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dcf4be-ea5a-5ab3-8919-e61da330fa85" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049617}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553534}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Source-Free Vector Fields"} :step/id #uuid "2c5a36f7-0cf5-56e0-9ad2-156e162e6abd" :step/math-academy-id 35868 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199319}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199318 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199478 :tutorial/content "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)= P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nwhich is defined on an open set $D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2.$ Recall that a function $\\psi(x,y)$ is said to be a **stream function** of $\\mathbf F$ if it satisfies\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad \nQ = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nIf a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ has a stream function $\\psi(x,y),$ then $\\mathbf{F}$ is called a **source-free vector field**. \n\nSource-free vector fields have some interesting properties, summarized below:\n\n- *The divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ at any point of $D$ is zero:* \n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0\n$$\n\n- *The *flux* of $\\mathbf{F}$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero:*\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = 0\n$$\n\n- *Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1, C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that*\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nStream functions for source-free vector fields play a similar role to potential functions for conservative vector fields. So it's worth comparing them side-by-side.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" colspan=\"2\">Source-Free</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"450\">Stream Function $\\psi$\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\mathbf F =  \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf i -  \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf j$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Relationship between $\\psi$ and $\\mathbf F$\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\mathbf F$ is tangential to $\\psi$'s level curves\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Divergence is zero\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Flux is zero along closed curves\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = 0$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Flux integrals are path independent\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s$\n</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td width=\"30\"></td>\n<td>\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" colspan=\"2\">Conservative</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"450\">Potential Function $\\phi$\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\mathbf F =  \\dfrac{\\partial \\phi}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf i +  \\dfrac{\\partial \\phi}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf j$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Relationship between $\\phi$ and $\\mathbf F$\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\mathbf F$ is perpendicular to $\\phi$'s level curves\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">(Scalar) curl is zero\n</td><td align=\"center\"> $\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Circulation is zero along closed curves\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf{r}= 0$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Line integrals are path independent\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf{r}$\n</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :tutorial/id #uuid "69005432-5588-5ac3-bf47-9dd734bb6953" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7703 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3e2deeb1-79f4-5a70-897d-e88547dd9d33" :step/math-academy-id 44699 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199328}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199319 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198403 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198045 :question/id #uuid "7a42fb45-176b-5550-a206-41a92859987b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12440"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd9aad0a-f415-577c-9cb0-15044e4a43fa" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050793}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553574}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux in a Source-Free Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "c4b926bf-0635-5002-9a4a-976c76662c96" :step/math-academy-id 44701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199328 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199485 :tutorial/content "Let's use what we know about source-free vector fields to calculate the flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2y\\,\\mathbf{i}-2x\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nacross the curve $C,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/d6/d6adda2809af5d49c6b3dd0e81eae913dcdf02ceec0ae919fda0011d8a3dad2b.png)\n\nFirst, notice that this is a source-free vector field, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x) \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\mathbf{F}$ is source-free, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across any closed curve is zero. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "447855fc-5a5c-5450-99a6-91de0ed2203b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7751 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "b606814b-0712-51ca-ad68-9fac2299c246" :step/math-academy-id 44869 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199265}}] :activity/title "Source-Free Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198492}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516886} :activity/id #uuid "e71fa85e-3388-5c09-b5c9-e68c6ca8665c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050224 :topic/id #uuid "37969d71-54e0-5479-8db0-03f8bc2bcfc3" :topic/math-academy-id 4160} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516886 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b2bbcab-d62a-5075-8355-9faf937b3b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193980} {:answer/id #uuid "978e1fe9-4852-59e9-8773-0093fead0b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193981} {:answer/id #uuid "9b10dc32-4033-5b4f-b063-1d903f627bfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193982} {:answer/id #uuid "c43a6e0c-13f2-5e06-a652-597bacfa0c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193983} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb45f0e-f0a4-5b16-a97f-a59ee8482aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193984}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193982} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf08e281-72a3-5471-856b-830ab0dddba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c7922961-2d09-5acf-9285-b8bc18d81065" :question/math-academy-id "q-126828" :question/problem "Given the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i-y\\,j,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $F$ is a source-free vector field\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0$ along any piecewise-smooth curve $C$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=x\\,i-y\\,j$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=-y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=1+(-1)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. This follows from statement I.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $F$ is source-free, the result ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0$ is guaranteed for closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "2c9dee19-233e-57e9-803b-852c86658342" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516887}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516887 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "939e78ae-4e2c-5ec0-a6f9-cb84365bf9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194383} {:answer/id #uuid "ca03740f-54af-5ef3-9423-bef53dce28f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Not enough information" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194384} {:answer/id #uuid "1a5b4489-073e-52d1-b96b-4d6a8829b185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194385} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a073ff-7b82-5cb3-a3ab-85a3bfc8115e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516525} {:answer/id #uuid "e94e1e83-b34b-5c19-b820-1bb79fe623cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516596}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186194383} :answer-field/id #uuid "36a3e99a-fa1b-516e-b9ce-8e1cc0722587" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e2446c5e-a91d-56d6-9926-c800e872256d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162216" :question/problem "![](images/4f/4f9cd7d9e173410de891756ee2ef260a16677629dacbfc56ab5e6e207a9e40e4.png)\n\nWhat is the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j$ across the curve $C$ shown above?\n\n*Hint: Start by checking whether $F$ is source-free.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is a source-free vector field. So, the flux of $F$ across any closed curve is zero.\nHence,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2ef43ced-14e4-5f9d-8129-931a0d00fe17" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516795 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f038cab-9126-5da7-9160-6ef0ca5d9c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516171} {:answer/id #uuid "80c57ef5-3745-587d-8aa3-f8f37a48d08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516383} {:answer/id #uuid "a789a160-6d54-5ae6-bea1-64c85a41f536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Not enough information" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516461} {:answer/id #uuid "c7a6dd0f-1b6a-5d10-8dec-872aaf97ae8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516534} {:answer/id #uuid "e865d1e4-4903-5b92-a7e7-c03d0e7b2809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516594}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516594} :answer-field/id #uuid "383d60a8-2215-591e-977a-5ba213748bef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "443c8318-6bcc-5f69-a92f-89796fbae407" :question/math-academy-id "q-162553" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c7d224dce33a51b94aa81c11ac4fae923c294e61372eb12d77e3656a0f61489.png)\n\nWhat is the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ across the curve $C$ shown above?\n\n*Hint: Start by checking whether $F$ is source-free.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is a source-free vector field. So, the flux of $F$ across any closed curve is zero.\nHence,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5930e567-37c7-540d-81dd-7080ad2f8497" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516914}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516914 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d564762-e4a4-5bb0-afe8-b8b8f6fac3d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516141} {:answer/id #uuid "b123ec58-423f-51d9-9afa-58d0e3e2907d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516480} {:answer/id #uuid "bb5bb2aa-9b98-5e14-a1e1-eb9f5bf08258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516506} {:answer/id #uuid "bc23addf-a23c-586d-8c9e-b7440595c852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516507} {:answer/id #uuid "be5c6d3f-c2f5-5f4b-a5b6-a35aad7bbaac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516506} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2781212-fedc-5b0a-8c49-342c3e418b01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c428bbdb-3474-5c6d-a12b-bbe7007eab5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162566" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f84859100733c56d6381f6ae5c3f5fa55c693621190b3f921e3e1e8f850a4f9.png)\n\nThe curve ${C}_{1}$ has endpoints $A(1,1)$ and $B(-1,-1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2x\\,i+(3-2y)\\,j$ through ${C}_{1}$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector ${n}_{1},$ as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $F$ is source-free, and the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ can be parametrized as*\n\n$$\nr=(1-2t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3-2y)=2-2=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular, as we have that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B,$ and ${n}_{2}$ is the unit normal to ${C}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5186fb872f03e73124d04c2e9ff07e77fb336bef121484a9ff1b697ea0cf8bcd.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nWe have the parametrization\n\n$$\nr(t)=(1-2t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =2x=2(1-2t)=2-4t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =3-2y=3-2(1-2t)=1+4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)=-2, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-4t)\\cdot (-2)-(1+4t)\\cdot (-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-4t)-(1+4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}1-8t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot {[t-4{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (1-4) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=6.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6a936763-e5a0-53ef-b650-fb6b24c25aaf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516950}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516950 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b595a3e-31c3-551c-aa15-2adbb1a368be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516133} {:answer/id #uuid "41921c02-93c9-559a-a5a1-ba1cc7f58824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516241} {:answer/id #uuid "88b0143e-7552-5d17-a22a-24748c27d899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516398} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b23766-cc77-57ee-8748-4e55410f3e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516459} {:answer/id #uuid "d99a11bb-26fc-599e-b35a-158a8e9b882d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516570} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d787048-8743-53b0-8f95-62517b984fc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bee8fa81-1bb7-5df3-bc77-fd80b7f5193e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162568" :question/problem "![](images/40/40e6a88e309f6ce5fece628a6d9bd66ffd00105edd3816fac04ae534ef7558b5.png)\n\nThe curve ${C}_{1}$ has endpoints $A(2,1)$ and $B(-1,-1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(4x+2y)\\,i-(8x+4y)\\,j$ through ${C}_{1}$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector ${n}_{1},$ as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $F$ is source-free, and the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ can be parametrized as*\n\n$$\nr=(2-3t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-8x-4y))=4-4=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular, as we have that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B,$ and ${n}_{2}$ is the unit normal to ${C}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/36/363ebd04d2629ed2b1b29ccab326e22737cf42e4c026d62910a50a1dd88e80f2.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nWe have the parametrization\n\n$$\nr(t)=(2-3t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =4x+2y=4(2-3t)+2(1-2t)=-16t+10, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-8x-4y=-8(2-3t)-4(1-2t)=32t-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-3t)=-3, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-16t+10)\\cdot (-2)-(32t-20)\\cdot (-3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(32t-20)+(96t-60)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}128t-80\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[64{t}^{2}-80t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =64-80 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-16.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b3437d82-8feb-504d-9712-8f8d4d548aa2"}] :activity/title "Source-Free Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516111}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046669} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553646} :activity/id #uuid "2d018b8f-2cb9-5071-bceb-06a7dbb334a1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050224 :topic/id #uuid "37969d71-54e0-5479-8db0-03f8bc2bcfc3" :topic/math-academy-id 4160} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c87aef5-ea40-593e-a9d5-aefa7bc0b47e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553066} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4143c5-4aaf-5240-a3bb-dd85ed36c046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553217} {:answer/id #uuid "cfad29ab-2014-585b-8cfe-0bd344b97623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553284} {:answer/id #uuid "ee223723-3c0a-530d-bdaa-ad54b0e416ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553338} {:answer/id #uuid "fd22e3e1-c3c5-5819-b13e-ae357d0d0b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Not enough information" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553369} :answer-field/id #uuid "de0560d7-2411-5cb7-8285-f3b1b348a142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1a74f654-b8c0-5b05-930c-8a74a2b8b3d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162549" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3b626ea7f1db9e9f7cf92441e3669cbca85a8d469515e5185e87d241440d1bde.png)\n\nWhat is the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ across the curve $C$ shown above?\n\n*Hint: Start by checking whether $F$ is source-free.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is *not* a source-free vector field. \n\nSince $F$ is not source-free, we do not have enough information to determine the flux of $F$ through $C.$"} :step/id #uuid "7efc6d5d-247b-5ec0-8dbf-afc93829600b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553641 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bd42f49-0ece-5ce9-8b34-6a707862e044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552949} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab526ed-224c-541f-a66d-92563c921b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552987} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccab4e0-07a0-5a0f-b2ca-1faf663c12d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{28}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553090} {:answer/id #uuid "7d16d138-325b-50ca-b473-15666930d48a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{76}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553122} {:answer/id #uuid "95d56263-122e-5c18-988e-3b958f4009d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553174}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552987} :answer-field/id #uuid "441e4f24-d3a1-5f34-b559-dd35657f1f13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d222c5e4-f6d8-59cd-b06f-5764984195b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-162651" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c7c48912df96585f7af01f3d0c1af904d1ca554555f7c63147fb5e0aa6676884.png)\n\nThe curve ${C}_{1}$ has endpoints $A(1,3)$ and $B(5,3).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=-\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}\\,i+(x-1)^{2}\\,j$ through ${C}_{1}$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector  ${n}_{1},$ as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $F$ is source-free.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{{y}^{3}})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-1)^{2})=0+0=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the straight line from $A(1,3)$ to $B(5,3)$ and ${n}_{2}$ is its unit normal, shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/3d/3d64ffb0e4cc313a2857d1c20922c1202619d0bdeb3d97f2808b4995c99f7f9f.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParametrizing ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =i+3\\,j+t\\cdot [(5\\,i+3\\,j)-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(4t+1)\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,1].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =-\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}=-\\sqrt{(3)^{3}}=-\\sqrt{27}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}=((4t+1)-1)^{2}=16{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+1)=4, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\sqrt{27})\\cdot (0)-(16{t}^{2})\\cdot (4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-64{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{64{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-\\frac{64}{3}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "84ca85c7-8f02-50ac-8aa5-148160d0bb9b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372097 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ea153ff-ca9a-53b6-87e8-2bc6a0c2501b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Not enough information" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372099} {:answer/id #uuid "84df548c-6bde-5a63-97d5-4fbf449c3966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372100} {:answer/id #uuid "a3202131-59f4-5fd9-9f90-b0703666599e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372101} {:answer/id #uuid "dfaf9c30-a918-58ab-9ce8-23b59599fcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372102} {:answer/id #uuid "6f2c2c57-5714-5fbf-9c4e-fdaf923dacc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372103}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372103} :answer-field/id #uuid "b38ccff0-d5a9-53de-b4dc-3a512c813886" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fe6d87cd-8e17-54ec-91d3-1c49d7e4c19b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162545" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1cac5607abe416bf2ba5b4a6af42315edcc898034331034de68dd4c9be151c5e.png)\n\nWhat is the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j$ across the curve $C$ shown above?\n\n*Hint: Start by checking whether $F$ is source-free.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,ds$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,ds={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,ds.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is a source-free vector field. So, the flux of $F$ across any closed curve is zero.\nHence,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,ds=0.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8bc7bf0d-8a57-5ed4-902b-c6da640ff1d4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553534 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "287d9505-ed14-5cee-90ec-50d38ff00887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552939} {:answer/id #uuid "471f6853-2555-57e6-a1c4-91e6fe90085d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553015} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca52f83-c8fb-58ed-8eda-01bc85761a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553035} {:answer/id #uuid "6f421748-53a9-541f-b31e-840a1b3dc0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553094} {:answer/id #uuid "e924ac84-61a3-537f-b4fd-6ebe6af690e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553331}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552939} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d52351a-5e24-5caf-bde1-3eef0cbcd908" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6f712531-9b84-53ea-bba8-2a30378e29b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162555" :question/problem "Given the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0$\n\nII. There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F$\n\nIII. $F$ is a source-free vector field" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+2x=4x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Indeed, this follows from statement I.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ec68a3c6-ceae-5e0c-a804-66d3a7e4a8fe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553683}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043a47af-d945-52f5-a2ff-8dbd6cbb4d74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552873} {:answer/id #uuid "084f29ee-31a1-58ac-a67f-c03a521c322a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552877} {:answer/id #uuid "0c339ee0-0900-5543-a85c-daf2f6f91a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552881} {:answer/id #uuid "1ba0bf79-9f84-57c9-91e2-72a01fc879ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552916} {:answer/id #uuid "e589549d-f5f7-5a08-a2a2-44640623c1a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553324}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552873} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e1b0569-2ef4-5afb-bff6-cf96b04525ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "27c3df67-079e-5e01-9714-60648afd83ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-162628" :question/problem "![](images/84/84cfc63faa3514c7b6151d5ab8f3f8e4e7deaafa8739fa637af2f0ce7453b7fb.png)\n\nThe curve ${C}_{1}$ has endpoints $A(1,1)$ and $B(5,4).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-2xy\\,j$ through ${C}_{1}$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector ${n}_{1},$ as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $F$ is source-free.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2xy)=2x-2x=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the straight line from $A(1,1)$ to $B(5,4)$ and  ${n}_{2}$ is its unit normal, shown in the diagram below. \n\n![](images/18/18b1a5bdc11ca3c2b4b728cf8df3a3e1c4b6c2fbbe86549e9ad773367495c533.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParametrizing ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =i+j+t\\cdot [(5\\,i+4j)-(i+j)] \\\\  & =(4t+1)\\,i+(3t+1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,1].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={x}^{2}=(4t+1)^{2}=16{t}^{2}+8t+1, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-2xy=-2(4t+1)(3t+1)=-24{t}^{2}-14t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+1)=4, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+1)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((16{t}^{2}+8t+1)\\cdot (3)-(-24{t}^{2}-14t-2)\\cdot (4))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\,144{t}^{2}+80t+11\\,)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[48{t}^{3}+40{t}^{2}+11t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(48+40+11) \\\\  & =99.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=99.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f7a4d50d-bcd8-51eb-8dbc-add455b212bf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553641}}] :activity/title "Source-Free Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552853}]}
